{- | `pds/serve.mdk` — the PDS entry point: admit a configuration, rehydrate the
   account repository from disk, bind the listener, and serve.

   Every configuration value arrives as a command-line flag or as a file at a
   fixed path, and is admitted before anything is bound: a bad DID,
   handle, or hostname is rejected by `lib.server_core`'s `makeAccount`, an
   unreadable or non-hex signing key by `readHexBytes`, a data directory that
   is not a directory by `requireDir`,
   and a repository whose head does not verify under the configured key by
   `lib.repo`'s `repoFromBlocks`. Every one of those exits nonzero. There is no
   default for any of them, so a misconfigured server never starts serving
   something other than what was asked for.

   The server listens on loopback, `127.0.0.1`, and on nothing else: the
   address is `shell.server`'s `listenAddress`, not a flag, so the listener's
   type names it and `medaka manifest` reports it. The only way to reach the
   server from off the box is the reverse proxy in front of it
   (`docs/ops/PDS-DEPLOY.md`). `--trusted-proxy` is separate from the address:
   it says whether that proxy's `X-Forwarded-For` names the client identity
   rate limiting keys on.

   The data directory is `data/`, relative to the working directory
   (`shell.datadir`), and no flag names it: it must exist before the server
   starts, and a run whose `data/` is missing or is not a directory is
   refused. Every function that reaches it takes a `DataDir`, so the rows name
   the `data/*` subtree and not the bare file labels.

   One data directory is served by one process. `data/` is locked
   (`shell.dirlock`) before anything beneath it is read or written, so a second
   server started over a live directory is refused rather than sweeping the
   staged files the first one has in flight. A killed process leaves its lock
   behind — `mkdir(2)` releases nothing on death — so the lock is graded by the
   holder's heartbeat and not by its existence, and an ordinary restart
   reclaims an abandoned one on its own. `--force-lock` skips that wait.

   The secrets live at fixed paths under `secrets/`, relative to the working
   directory, and no flag names them: `secrets/key.hex`, `secrets/token.hex`
   and `secrets/password`. Each path is written out literally at the call that
   reads it, so the rows of the functions that read them name exactly those
   files.

   The signing key, `secrets/key.hex`, is required. It is read here, admitted
   as a `lib.sign.SecretKey` by the same function that reads it, and handed to
   `lib.repo`, which is the only thing that needs it, and to nothing else. No
   binding in this file or in `pds/shell` holds the key's bytes past that read.
   It is never printed, and neither is the `Store` that transitively holds it.

   The session-token secret is the SECOND secret this entry point holds: the
   server's own 32-byte HS256 key, which `lib.server_core`'s seam verifies
   bearer tokens against. It is deliberately a different key from the signing
   key: the signing key authenticates commits to every other implementation on
   the network and has exactly one job. `secrets/token.hex` supplies it when
   present; when absent, the secret is generated from `osEntropyBytes` on first
   run and kept in `data/session-secret` for every run after.

   The account password is the THIRD, and it is what `com.atproto.server.
   createSession` grades a login against. It arrives in `secrets/password` and
   NEVER as an argument value: an argument is visible in `ps` output to every
   user on the box. There is no interactive prompt either — no termios, tty, or
   echo-suppression primitive exists in the runtime or the stdlib, and a prompt
   that echoed the password to the terminal would be worse than the file, not
   better. The password is read once, by a run whose data directory holds no
   credential yet, turned into a PBKDF2 credential record (`lib.credential`),
   and never stored: `data/credential` holds a salt, an iteration count, and
   a derived key. Once that record exists `secrets/password` must be deleted: a
   run that finds both refuses to start rather than leave a plaintext copy of
   the password beside the credential derived from it.

   Every file this program creates holding a secret — the generated session
   secret and the credential record — is written at mode 0600, and a secret
   file it READS at any wider mode is refused before the listener binds rather
   than used: a signing key or session-token secret that another account on
   the box can read is not a secret, and serving anyway would be
   indistinguishable from a correctly permissioned deployment right up until
   somebody used it.

   A failed configuration leaves nothing behind either: every secret `secrets/`
   SUPPLIES is read and admitted before any secret this program GENERATES is
   written, so a run rejected for a bad password file leaves no session secret
   on disk for the next run to adopt. That invariant covers the durable EVENT
   LOG as well as the secrets: recovering a half-finished append is decided
   with the rest of the admissions and performed only after the last step that
   can still refuse, so a refused run mutates the log no more than it mutates
   the secrets. -}

